</div>
</section>
<section id="sec2" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">2</span>Where the record's order and its entries differ</h2>
<p>The boot note's order after tonight: the rest of the consolidation (POSITIONS, perhaps INDEX's form), the writer's brief shown to you, the writer's draft, the check against the sort's list, the cold read, your reading, <code>cloud-container</code>, batch 14.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The trial boot.</strong> Your goals say the boot question is settled on evidence and a trial boot, not assumed. The question is how FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX are kept, and which are always loaded and which looked up. The boot note's order has no trial boot. The plan adds it as step 8, for your decision.</li>
<li><strong>The modes first.</strong> Your goals say the coordinator's modes are identified and described first. The boot note's order names no such step. The plan puts the candidate modes in the writer's brief, so you see them before the writer runs.</li>
<li><strong>POSITIONS tonight.</strong> Your words at 01:37 covered "the rest of FACTS and POSITIONS the same way". The boot note holds POSITIONS for your morning, because its entries are your decisions in your words and you haven't seen the method applied to them.</li>
<li><strong>The lint.</strong> Your order of 10-12 was the moves, then the consolidation with a new lint for the record, then the brief. At 21:00 you weren't convinced by the lint. Tonight's slices run without it.</li>
<li><strong>After the cold read.</strong> POSITIONS has the coordinator review the flags and the writer fix them before you read. The boot note's order skips that. The plan follows POSITIONS, in steps 9 to 11.</li>
<li><strong>What the writer writes from.</strong> Your goals say the writer works from the sort. The same entry says the two sorts carry the map's short names, not the rules' texts, and aren't what the writer judges a rule by. The boot note of 10-12 16:34 reads it as the rule texts in POSITIONS and the protocol. The brief follows that note.</li>
<li><strong>The drops.</strong> Both sorts drop rules whose failures are on record: the clock, the closed-decision search, delegating, rewriting the boot note whole, and silence during a batch. Sort A also drops watching a long run's spend. Your later sentence keeps a rule when its failure is on record, and lets the interview drop nothing. The plan follows your sentence.</li>
<li><strong>The full boot read.</strong> The README and POSITIONS's header say FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX are read whole at boot, by your word of 09-30. Your goals reopen what's loaded and what's looked up. The trial boot is where the two meet.</li>
<li><strong>The clock and the models.</strong> The boot note says the clock rule is for Fable, and that Fable's interview answer didn't mention the clock. Fable's per-turn answer does mention it. Only its one-shot answer doesn't. Tonight's slips came under Opus. The rule's failures are on record under both.</li>
<li><strong>One file or tiers.</strong> On 10-07 and 10-10 you leaned to one skill file loaded whole. Your goals, written later, make it tiered: a core and mode parts. The later entry stands, so this needs no decision.</li>
</ol>
<div class="refs"><p class="refs-title">References</p>
<ol>
<li>POSITIONS, "The coordinator rewrite's goals."; BOOT.md, "The order after tonight".</li>
<li>The same goals entry ("the coordinator's modes are identified and described first").</li>
<li>BOOT.md, the night's work, his words at 01:37, and step 4.</li>
<li>The boot note of 10-12 16:34 ("The order he set"; the proposed lint "that flags entries over a cap ... and entries carrying a date, a hash, a rung name"); HELD, chat, 21:00; <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/</code> has no such script.</li>
<li>POSITIONS, "The skills are written for a reader new to the repository ..."; BOOT.md, "The order after tonight".</li>
<li>POSITIONS, "The coordinator rewrite's goals." (its last two sentences); the boot note of 10-12 16:34 ("Decided, in POSITIONS").</li>
