<h2><span class="num">4</span>Your decisions</h2>
<p>Each has a default you can take without reading the argument.</p>
<p class="group">For the record-keeping part:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>POSITIONS's pass.</strong> Answered: it runs in step 3, by the record-keeping part, after the cold read.</li>
<li><strong>What FACTS and POSITIONS both tell.</strong> POSITIONS keeps the decision and a pointer, and FACTS the state of the build, as proposed on 10-12. Or both keep their text. Default: the proposal. It touches more than a third of each file.</li>
<li><strong>INDEX's form.</strong> Group its lines by topic now, for about 0.5M to 1M, an estimate. Or leave it until the trial boot shows how INDEX is read. Default: leave it. You kept it loaded on 09-30.</li>
<li><strong>A length check.</strong> A script that flags an entry over the part's measure, beside the check that nothing was lost. Or a reader's check alone. Default: the script, since a measure a script checks holds where a reader's judgement drifted in step 1.</li>
</ol>
<p class="group">Before the writer runs:</p>
<ol start="5">
<li><strong>Where the trial boot goes.</strong> After the draft and the check, testing the draft's boot (default). Or before the writer, testing the record's boot read alone. Each costs about 0.7M, an estimate. After the draft, if the boot fails, its part is rewritten for about 0.1M, an estimate.</li>
<li><strong>The modes.</strong> The candidates in the brief, or your own list. Default: the brief's list, which the writer may merge or split.</li>
<li><strong>Five rules no failure search covered.</strong> A reversible stop doesn't hold a batch. A fork is probed before a batch is briefed. FACTS is read whole at boot. Boot reads no directory listings. What's in flight is read from the tree. Default: keep all five, since the interview alone drops nothing. A Sonnet scout could trace their failures for about 0.5M, an estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Four rules the scout found no failure for.</strong> Label which part of a reply answers which. Take a step a yes covers without asking again. Search the record for how it was done last time. A fact enters in the commit that establishes it. Default: drop them, and fold the third into the kept search, since the 16:43 failure was a search that didn't fire before measuring.</li>
<li><strong>Your questions on origins.</strong> You asked where four rules came from: one ask per message, "he decides what is committed", silence on record edits, and new terms against ASD-STE100. Default: answered from each rule's POSITIONS text, as you asked at 16:00. A Sonnet scout for the four origins costs about 0.5M, an estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Your role.</strong> "He sets the goals and decides the design and process questions put to him", in place of "decides what is committed". Default: that wording, from the held list.</li>
<li><strong>Record edits.</strong> You hear nothing. Or you hear "noted". Or you get a one-line read-back that catches a dictation slip, which both models give by default. Default: "noted", your own word.</li>
<li><strong>The clock rule's wording.</strong> "Read the clock before writing any time", the boot note's line since Opus wrote it on 10-06. Or the 09-23 wording, with its reason that the model has no feel for elapsed time. You asked for "the formulation that worked", and the record doesn't say which. Default: the boot note's line. Tonight's slips happened with it in context.</li>
<li><strong>Holding a long dictated message.</strong> The held list as it is, a line per point with your message's time. Or the models' default, a numbered list with a status per point. Default: the held list as it is.</li>
<li><strong>Two lessons with no rule.</strong> No do-nothing option among the choices (10-12, 16:45). A brief for an update caps its length against the version before (10-13). A new rule enters only at your word. Default: one line each, in the asking and the briefing parts.</li>
<li><strong>git log at boot.</strong> One line in the boot order, or none. Default: one line, as sort A says. Both models do it, and the boot note can lag a worker's push.</li>
<li><strong>A message when a batch starts.</strong> None, and silence until you ask. Or one line, as both models would. Default: none, as you said at 15:38.</li>
<li><strong>POSITIONS's two conduct sections in the boot read</strong> once the skill holds their rules. Default: they stay until the trial boot says otherwise. POSITIONS keeps your words, and the skill the rule.</
